2
00:00:11,632 --> 00:00:16,929
Software engineers have a delightful practice called rubber ducking.

3
00:00:17,722 --> 00:00:20,516
When they run into a problem with their code.

4
00:00:20,516 --> 00:00:23,227
They'll explain it to a rubber duck.

5
00:00:24,228 --> 00:00:27,648
They'll work step by step, line by line,

6
00:00:28,316 --> 00:00:31,277
and some of the time they'll learn something new.

7
00:00:32,361 --> 00:00:34,030
But how can this work?

8
00:00:34,864 --> 00:00:36,282
The ducks don't talk back,

9
00:00:36,949 --> 00:00:38,993
so the programmers are explaining

10
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:44,457
to themselves they're not getting any new information.

11
00:00:45,583 --> 00:00:50,463
And yet something about this process helps them learn.

12
00:00:51,631 --> 00:00:56,886
Rubber ducking is an example of what I call learning by thinking

13
00:00:57,970 --> 00:01:02,183
and understanding how learning by thinking works is the key

14
00:01:02,183 --> 00:01:04,018
to becoming a better learner.

15
00:01:05,269 --> 00:01:07,939
Now most of us want to be better learners.

16
00:01:08,606 --> 00:01:11,776
That's because we want to make our minds better, more effective,

17
00:01:11,776 --> 00:01:12,860
and more informed.

18
00:01:13,903 --> 00:01:17,490
But learning can be hard, and in the years ahead,

19
00:01:17,490 --> 00:01:20,952
we'll have access to more and more tools and to more

20
00:01:20,952 --> 00:01:25,748
and more information through the internet and social media and AI

21
00:01:26,916 --> 00:01:32,255
and more information can make learning harder rather than easier.

22
00:01:33,256 --> 00:01:36,300
We run the risk of information overwhelm.

23
00:01:37,593 --> 00:01:39,762
Part of the problem is that we tend

24
00:01:39,762 --> 00:01:45,685
to approach learning by looking outside of ourselves for more information,

25
00:01:46,727 --> 00:01:49,939
but that can just exacerbate the overwhelm.

26
00:01:50,940 --> 00:01:51,941
Instead,

27
00:01:52,775 --> 00:01:54,902
we could be learning by thinking.

28
00:01:54,902 --> 00:01:58,823
We could be starting with the information we already have.

29
00:02:00,241 --> 00:02:04,912
I'm a cognitive scientist and I've been studying learning for over 25 years.

30
00:02:05,746 --> 00:02:09,250
One thing I've come to appreciate is that our minds are richer

31
00:02:09,667 --> 00:02:12,753
and deeper than we often give them credit for.

32
00:02:13,504 --> 00:02:17,675
In fact, our minds contain vast repositories of knowledge.

33
00:02:18,176 --> 00:02:22,138
Knowledge that's contained in our memories and our skills.

34
00:02:23,181 --> 00:02:26,976
Learning by thinking is the process of tapping into that knowledge

35
00:02:27,310 --> 00:02:28,853
to become a better learner.

36
00:02:29,979 --> 00:02:32,982
To give you a sense for learning by thinking in action,

37
00:02:32,982 --> 00:02:34,984
I'd like to ask you a few questions.

38
00:02:35,568 --> 00:02:38,487
As soon as you have an answer, please raise your hand.

39
00:02:39,363 --> 00:02:40,364
You ready?

40
00:02:42,783 --> 00:02:48,456
How many windows are along the front of your house or apartment?

41
00:02:55,796 --> 00:02:59,175
What is the fifth letter of the alphabet?

42
00:03:05,181 --> 00:03:11,103
Is the letter Z on the left or the right side of a typical keyboard?

43
00:03:17,568 --> 00:03:21,072
I'll bet that many of you didn't know the answers

44
00:03:21,072 --> 00:03:23,449
to these questions immediately,

45
00:03:24,408 --> 00:03:27,495
but you had the knowledge you needed to figure them out.

46
00:03:28,329 --> 00:03:30,248
You could use your visual memories

47
00:03:30,414 --> 00:03:33,918
to count the number of windows along the front of your home.

48
00:03:33,918 --> 00:03:35,878
You could use your auditory memories

49
00:03:36,003 --> 00:03:39,757
to figure out that E is the fifth letter of the alphabet,

50
00:03:39,757 --> 00:03:43,552
and you could use your motor skills to figure out that the letter Z

51
00:03:43,719 --> 00:03:46,681
on the left side of a typical keyboard.

52
00:03:47,807 --> 00:03:50,810
These simple examples highlight the richness

53
00:03:50,851 --> 00:03:54,146
and depth of the knowledge you already have,

54
00:03:54,897 --> 00:03:58,025
and they illustrate the process of learning by thinking.

55
00:03:59,652 --> 00:04:03,739
But recognizing that you have untapped knowledge is not enough

56
00:04:03,739 --> 00:04:06,033
to become a better learner.

57
00:04:06,033 --> 00:04:09,912
That's because you also need to know how to extract that knowledge

58
00:04:10,246 --> 00:04:11,247
when you need it.

59
00:04:12,957 --> 00:04:16,043
Suppose you decide to bake chocolate chip cookies,

60
00:04:16,961 --> 00:04:20,548
but the recipe calls for chocolate chips and for salted butter,

61
00:04:20,923 --> 00:04:22,967
neither of which you already have.

62
00:04:24,302 --> 00:04:28,014
Fortunately, you can transform that bar of chocolate you do have

63
00:04:28,264 --> 00:04:31,267
into the chocolate pieces you need for the recipe,

64
00:04:31,267 --> 00:04:35,396
and you can combine unsalted butter with salt to get salted butter.

65
00:04:35,938 --> 00:04:39,525
This is probably familiar when we're cooking,

66
00:04:39,817 --> 00:04:43,571
we often transform and combine the ingredients we have

67
00:04:44,238 --> 00:04:46,073
to get the ingredients we need.

68
00:04:47,116 --> 00:04:50,870
It's just the same for learning when we're learning by thinking.

69
00:04:51,162 --> 00:04:57,877
We transform and combine the information we already have to see how this works.

70
00:04:58,169 --> 00:05:02,298
Consider that information can be represented in all sorts of ways.

71
00:05:02,298 --> 00:05:07,386
Until today, you probably represented the windows along the front of your home,

72
00:05:07,887 --> 00:05:12,850
not in the form of a single number, but as a series of visual memories.

73
00:05:13,976 --> 00:05:18,856
So when you transform and combine those memories to extract a single number,

74
00:05:19,106 --> 00:05:20,399
you learn something new.

75
00:05:20,816 --> 00:05:24,570
You learn how many windows along the front of your home.

76
00:05:24,570 --> 00:05:28,991
And so you can answer a question that you couldn't answer before.

77
00:05:29,992 --> 00:05:32,703
That's the magic of learning by thinking.

78
00:05:34,121 --> 00:05:35,831
There is a catch, though.

79
00:05:36,415 --> 00:05:39,001
Not all learning can be done by thinking alone.

80
00:05:39,710 --> 00:05:41,796
If I've never been to your home.

81
00:05:42,046 --> 00:05:45,216
I can't think my way to how many windows you have.

82
00:05:45,466 --> 00:05:47,176
I need new information.

83
00:05:48,386 --> 00:05:53,974
But asking myself how many windows you have sets me up to learn so that

84
00:05:53,974 --> 00:05:58,771
when I do make it to your home, I know precisely what information to look for.

85
00:06:00,106 --> 00:06:03,818
That's why learning by thinking is so often the best place to start.

86
00:06:04,652 --> 00:06:07,029
Going back to our chocolate chip cookies,

87
00:06:07,029 --> 00:06:10,241
it makes sense to look at the ingredients you have already

88
00:06:10,658 --> 00:06:13,202
before you start making a shopping list.

89
00:06:14,787 --> 00:06:19,917
Unfortunately though, when it comes to learning, we don't have a recipe.

90
00:06:20,876 --> 00:06:25,089
There's no single strategy for learning that guarantees the best outcome

91
00:06:25,089 --> 00:06:29,510
in every case. But there's one strategy that comes close.

92
00:06:30,428 --> 00:06:35,891
It's a version of learning by thinking that involves seeking explanations.

93
00:06:37,643 --> 00:06:41,105
In the late 1980s, scientists wanted

94
00:06:41,105 --> 00:06:45,484
to understand what it is that differentiates the most successful

95
00:06:45,484 --> 00:06:48,863
students from their less successful peers.

96
00:06:49,822 --> 00:06:53,534
Even when students have access to exactly the same information,

97
00:06:53,534 --> 00:06:57,830
the same textbooks, the same passage of text on an exam.

98
00:06:58,581 --> 00:07:01,959
How much they learn can vary enormously.

99
00:07:03,252 --> 00:07:07,882
So what sets those best learners apart can't just be the information

100
00:07:07,882 --> 00:07:10,551
available outside their heads.

101
00:07:11,260 --> 00:07:14,972
It has to be something about what they do with that information.

102
00:07:16,390 --> 00:07:21,270
The scientists found that many of the most successful students had something

103
00:07:21,270 --> 00:07:22,271
in common.

104
00:07:23,272 --> 00:07:27,318
As they studied, they explained to themselves.

105
00:07:28,527 --> 00:07:34,033
They asked themselves questions, and they did their best to offer good replies.

106
00:07:35,242 --> 00:07:37,703
They were their own rubber ducks.

107
00:07:39,455 --> 00:07:43,501
Since then, lots of studies have found that explaining

108
00:07:43,542 --> 00:07:48,255
to yourself can have a dramatic impact on learning across a range of contexts

109
00:07:48,255 --> 00:07:49,381
and a range of domains.

110
00:07:50,257 --> 00:07:54,094
When children or adults explain to themselves,

111
00:07:54,929 --> 00:07:56,472
they tend to learn better

112
00:07:57,181 --> 00:08:01,685
and can apply what they learned more broadly than those who don't explain.

113
00:08:03,145 --> 00:08:06,315
This is known as the Self-explanation effect,

114
00:08:06,774 --> 00:08:10,110
and it is a powerful strategy for learning by thinking.

115
00:08:12,154 --> 00:08:15,074
Let me give you an example from my own research.

116
00:08:15,074 --> 00:08:19,620
It's an example that shows how children can transform the way

117
00:08:19,620 --> 00:08:22,873
they represent information by explaining.

118
00:08:24,166 --> 00:08:26,752
Just for fun. We'll illustrate with ducks.

119
00:08:27,711 --> 00:08:31,048
I'd like you to imagine a card with two ducks on it.

120
00:08:32,341 --> 00:08:34,468
We'll call it the reference card.

121
00:08:35,469 --> 00:08:37,555
Now imagine two new cards.

122
00:08:38,264 --> 00:08:43,269
One has two frogs, the other has one duck and one fox.

123
00:08:44,436 --> 00:08:49,316
You have to choose which of the two new cards goes with the reference card.

124
00:08:50,943 --> 00:08:53,946
Take a moment to consider which you would choose.

125
00:08:57,533 --> 00:09:02,830
Most research, including my own, finds that in a case like this one,

126
00:09:03,455 --> 00:09:07,334
adults will typically choose the card with two frogs to go

127
00:09:07,376 --> 00:09:08,919
with a card with two ducks.

128
00:09:09,920 --> 00:09:15,384
That's because they don't only represent the cards as having a frog and a frog

129
00:09:15,718 --> 00:09:16,927
and a duck and a duck.

130
00:09:17,928 --> 00:09:23,017
They also represent them as having the same objects and the same objects.

131
00:09:23,851 --> 00:09:25,978
When you represent the cards in this way,

132
00:09:26,604 --> 00:09:30,399
it's natural to pair the two sets of matching objects together.

133
00:09:32,067 --> 00:09:33,152
So what about kids?

134
00:09:34,445 --> 00:09:39,575
Five and six year old children will typically choose the card with one duck

135
00:09:39,575 --> 00:09:43,162
and one fox to go with a card with two ducks.

136
00:09:44,580 --> 00:09:49,668
That’s because they represent the reference card as having duck and a duck.

137
00:09:50,461 --> 00:09:54,882
So a card with one duck on it will be more similar to it than a card

138
00:09:54,882 --> 00:09:56,216
with no ducks at all.

139
00:09:56,216 --> 00:09:58,802
Will children do this even

140
00:09:58,802 --> 00:10:03,015
when they're shown plenty of examples to make it clearer that in this task,

141
00:10:03,807 --> 00:10:07,936
the right answer is the adult pattern of matching same with same.

142
00:10:09,355 --> 00:10:10,814
But in one of our studies,

143
00:10:11,273 --> 00:10:13,108
we were able to make five

144
00:10:13,108 --> 00:10:19,323
and six year old children think like adults by prompting them to explain.

145
00:10:20,449 --> 00:10:25,371
After seeing examples equivalent to the card with two frogs being paired

146
00:10:25,371 --> 00:10:27,581
with a card with two ducks.

147
00:10:27,581 --> 00:10:33,420
We prompted half the children to explain why those cards went together.

148
00:10:34,755 --> 00:10:40,511
We didn't give them any feedback, so they didn't have any new information yet.

149
00:10:40,552 --> 00:10:44,848
Those kids, who explained went on to pair new sets of cards,

150
00:10:44,848 --> 00:10:47,643
just like adults matching same

151
00:10:47,851 --> 00:10:53,440
with same by explaining they changed the way they represented

152
00:10:53,440 --> 00:10:55,859
the information to solve the task.

153
00:10:58,278 --> 00:11:00,656
Explaining has another remarkable property.

154
00:11:00,989 --> 00:11:03,158
It's one I've hinted at already.

155
00:11:03,158 --> 00:11:05,411
Explaining sets you up to learn.

156
00:11:06,745 --> 00:11:12,626
My friend Roxanna has a young son who asked her one day how tornadoes start.

157
00:11:13,502 --> 00:11:16,839
She'd taken a class on climate and natural disasters in college,

158
00:11:16,839 --> 00:11:19,758
so she thought she could explain it pretty well.

159
00:11:19,758 --> 00:11:22,720
She started to describe how there's a difference in pressure,

160
00:11:22,720 --> 00:11:25,097
and then she drew a blank.

161
00:11:26,390 --> 00:11:28,308
What sets it off? She didn't know.

162
00:11:28,976 --> 00:11:32,020
How exactly does low pressure produce a funnel?

163
00:11:32,020 --> 00:11:33,021
She couldn't say.

164
00:11:34,022 --> 00:11:38,527
She told me that she realized she knew nothing.

165
00:11:40,696 --> 00:11:45,659
I suspect that all of us have had an experience like this one.

166
00:11:46,910 --> 00:11:51,290
An experience of trying to explain something and realizing,

167
00:11:51,290 --> 00:11:55,878
the course of doing so that we understand much less than we thought we did.

168
00:11:57,254 --> 00:12:02,176
It's a phenomenon called the illusion of explanatory depth.

169
00:12:03,594 --> 00:12:07,222
And this illusion points to another reason why it's

170
00:12:07,222 --> 00:12:10,809
so valuable to start the process of learning from within

171
00:12:11,268 --> 00:12:13,520
with the information we have already.

172
00:12:14,730 --> 00:12:18,400
When we start to transform and combine what we know

173
00:12:19,026 --> 00:12:22,696
or what we think we know to craft an explanation,

174
00:12:23,739 --> 00:12:28,494
we often uncover gaps that point us to the information we're missing.

175
00:12:29,620 --> 00:12:31,830
So we know what questions to ask

176
00:12:32,122 --> 00:12:35,626
and how to connect the answers to what we know already.

177
00:12:36,752 --> 00:12:40,839
By trying to explain, Roxana identified a gap,

178
00:12:40,839 --> 00:12:45,052
something she needed to know how it is that low pressure relates

179
00:12:45,052 --> 00:12:46,303
to a tornado's funnel.

180
00:12:47,346 --> 00:12:50,766
She knew what needed to go on her information shopping list,

181
00:12:52,684 --> 00:12:55,103
knowing what we're missing.

182
00:12:55,813 --> 00:13:02,236
What to look for can be a first step in navigating information overwhelm.

183
00:13:03,695 --> 00:13:06,615
That's crucial because in the years ahead,

184
00:13:07,032 --> 00:13:10,118
that overwhelm will only become worse.

185
00:13:11,036 --> 00:13:16,750
Tools getting and synthesizing information will be all the more pervasive,

186
00:13:17,543 --> 00:13:21,129
and will be tempted to solve problems by turning first

187
00:13:21,338 --> 00:13:24,550
to the information available outside our heads.

188
00:13:25,467 --> 00:13:30,514
We'll be tempted to rely on AI generated responses to our search queries

189
00:13:31,098 --> 00:13:33,767
and on the explanations we can get from chatbots.

190
00:13:34,893 --> 00:13:40,816
These are incredible tools, but they shouldn’t replace learning by thinking.

191
00:13:41,650 --> 00:13:44,778
If we don't start with the information we have already,

192
00:13:45,571 --> 00:13:48,365
we outsource the process of transforming

193
00:13:48,365 --> 00:13:53,579
and combining that information that sets us up to seek the right information

194
00:13:54,204 --> 00:13:56,206
and to make sense of that information.

195
00:13:56,206 --> 00:13:57,207
When we get it,

196
00:13:58,333 --> 00:14:03,714
we become poor at policing the boundaries between what we know inside our heads

197
00:14:04,298 --> 00:14:07,509
and what we merely have access to outside them.

198
00:14:08,760 --> 00:14:13,307
We fall prey to illusions of understanding.

199
00:14:15,559 --> 00:14:19,313
But now you know how to break free from those illusions

200
00:14:20,898 --> 00:14:22,524
by seeking explanations.

201
00:14:23,775 --> 00:14:25,611
When you explain to yourself,

202
00:14:26,111 --> 00:14:31,909
you transform and combine the information you have to answer new questions.

203
00:14:33,744 --> 00:14:35,537
When you explain to yourself,

204
00:14:36,038 --> 00:14:39,875
you're more likely to recognize what it is you don't know.

205
00:14:39,875 --> 00:14:41,627
And so you know what to look for

206
00:14:41,919 --> 00:14:44,963
when it's time to shift from learning by thinking

207
00:14:44,963 --> 00:14:46,924
to learning from new information.

208
00:14:48,675 --> 00:14:52,930
Unfortunately, learning by thinking will not make learning easy,

209
00:14:53,972 --> 00:14:56,058
but it means your effort isn't wasted.

210
00:14:56,475 --> 00:15:00,437
It gives you a path through information, overwhelm to insight.

211
00:15:01,772 --> 00:15:05,192
And learning by thinking won't eliminate the need for new information,

212
00:15:05,192 --> 00:15:07,152
at least in lots of cases.

213
00:15:08,111 --> 00:15:10,197
But it helps you know where to look.

214
00:15:10,197 --> 00:15:15,285
It helps you know what you're missing and what to do with new information

215
00:15:15,452 --> 00:15:16,453
when you get it.

216
00:15:17,621 --> 00:15:19,289
So to be a better learner,

217
00:15:20,207 --> 00:15:23,377
don't start with the information outside

218
00:15:23,377 --> 00:15:25,045
your head.

219
00:15:25,087 --> 00:15:27,381
Look inside first.

220
00:15:28,340 --> 00:15:30,300
Take a moment.

221
00:15:32,344 --> 00:15:34,846
To ask yourself questions.

222
00:15:35,889 --> 00:15:38,392
To explain to yourself.

223
00:15:39,685 --> 00:15:45,148
Be your own rubber duck. Thank you.
